## The Bug

The crash happens on this line:

```javascript
const city = order.shipping_address.city;
```

`order.shipping_address` is `undefined` (or `null`) for at least some production orders, so trying to read `.city` on it throws `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')`.

**Why it works on your machine:** your local/test orders always have a fully populated `shipping_address`. In production, some orders don't — e.g. digital-only orders, orders where the customer hasn't filled in an address yet, or records created by a legacy/migration path that omits the nested address object.

## The Fix

Use optional chaining so that a missing `shipping_address` (or a missing `order` itself) simply yields `undefined` for `city`, which then falls through to the `"standard"` default:

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function getShippingZone(order) {
    const city = order?.shipping_address?.city;
    const zones = {
        "New York": "east",
        "Los Angeles": "west",
        "Chicago": "central",
    };
    return zones[city] || "standard";
}
</solution>

With this change, if `order` is `null`/`undefined`, or `order.shipping_address` is missing, `city` becomes `undefined`, `zones[undefined]` is `undefined`, and the function safely returns `"standard"` instead of crashing.

